# ================================================================== PART 1 · THE WHOLE THING AT A GLANCE
flow(id="big", part=0, scope="world",
 title="A Market for Pieces of Companies",
 kick="A stock market is a place where part-ownership of companies is sold once by the company and then resold, over and over, between strangers.",
 sum="A stock market does two jobs. It lets a company raise money by selling pieces of itself to the public. Then it lets the people who bought those pieces sell them on to someone else. The first job happens once in a company's life, or a few times. The second happens millions of times a day, and it is what the news means by the market.<br><br>This map explains how that machine works. It starts with the whole picture: what a share is, how many companies have them, where they are traded and how large it all is. Later parts go inside: the products, the way a company gets in, the path of a single trade, how a price forms and how it is pushed, and what happens in a crash. The first three parts are mechanics only. Nothing is argued until the parts have names.<br><br>To move through the story, use the buttons at the bottom of the screen: the arrows go one slide forward or back, and Play runs the slides on their own. Each slide moves the map and puts its text in the panel on the right.",
 bridge="The first slide says what a share is. The second separates the one sale that pays the company from all the sales that do not. The third counts the listed companies in each country. The fourth shows where the trading happens, exchange by exchange. The fifth compares the size of each market with the size of its economy.",
 out="A share is a slice of a company, the company is paid only when the slice is first sold, and the reselling is concentrated in a few places. The next chapter asks what a share pays and what sets its price.",
 rec="A share is a slice of a company. The company is paid for it once. Everything after that is resale, and nearly half of it by value sits on two exchanges in New York.",
 roles=[],
 src=[{"t": "US Securities and Exchange Commission, Investor.gov: Stocks", "u": STOCKS},
      {"t": "World Federation of Exchanges, Market Statistics, November 2026 (figures for September 2026)", "u": WFE},
      {"t": "UN Sustainable Stock Exchanges initiative, Stock Exchange Database", "u": SSE},
      {"t": "World Bank, Listed domestic companies, total", "u": WBD + "CM.MKT.LDOM.NO"},
      {"t": "World Bank, Market capitalization of listed domestic companies (per cent of GDP)", "u": WBD + "CM.MKT.LCAP.GD.ZS"}],
 steps=[
  st("Today", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "A Share Is a Slice of a Company",
   "A company can divide its ownership into equal slices. Each slice is a share, also called a stock. If a company has a million shares and you hold a thousand, you own one thousandth of it. The investor guide of the United States securities regulator describes what the ordinary kind, called common stock, gives its owner: a vote at shareholder meetings and a right to dividends, which are payments a company makes to its owners out of profit when its directors decide to pay them. A share does not give you a right to walk in and take a thousandth of the stock room. It gives you a claim on what the company earns and on what would be left if it were sold and its debts paid. Your loss is limited to what you paid for the share. Those three things, a vote, a possible payment and a claim on what is left, are all that is bought and sold on every exchange on this map.",
   STOCKS, w=1, pl=["sec"], note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country, because it defines a term used everywhere. The ringed circle is the United States Securities and Exchange Commission, whose investor guide it draws on. The small dots are the stock exchanges, explained three slides on."),
  st("Today", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "Two Markets: The First Sale, and Every Sale After",
   "Shares are sold in two different ways, and the difference matters for everything later. In the first, the company itself sells new shares and receives the money. That is the primary market. In the second, one investor sells existing shares to another, and the company receives nothing. That is the secondary market. Take a company that sells one million new shares at 20 each. It receives 20 million, once. If those shares later change hands at 50, the extra 30 goes to whoever sold them. The company's bank balance does not move. The scale of the two is very different. Professor Jay Ritter of the University of Florida counts 90 ordinary company flotations in the United States in 2025, raising 39 billion dollars. The exchanges of the Americas reported 13.3 trillion dollars of share trading in the single month of September 2026. So when a share price rises, the company has not been handed money. Its existing owners have become richer on paper.",
   RIT, w=1, ex=["ritter"], note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country. The diamond marks the University of Florida, where the count of flotations is kept. The trading figure is the World Federation of Exchanges total for its member exchanges in the Americas."),
  st("Latest year", "Every country with a figure", WORLD, 1.15, "How Many Companies Are Listed, Country by Country",
   "A company whose shares can be bought on an exchange is called listed. The World Bank publishes the number of listed domestic companies for %(ldom_n)s countries, using figures collected by the exchanges' own federation. Its world total for 2025 is 53,087. China has 11,149, India 8,510, Japan 3,942 and the United States 3,908. In %(ldom_low)s of the countries shaded here the number is under 50. Set that beside the number of businesses in any country, which runs into the millions, and the first fact about the stock market appears: almost no companies are in it. The ones that are tend to be the largest employers, banks, miners and manufacturers a country has. This is why a market with a few hundred names can still matter to everyone. The price of those few companies decides what pension funds are worth, what executives are paid and which firms can buy which.",
   WBD + "CM.MKT.LDOM.NO", data={"k": "ldom", "only": None}, pl=["wfe"]),
  st("September 2026", "Every exchange in the two sources", [-38, 34], 1.25, "Where the Trading Happens: The Exchanges",
   "An exchange is the organised market where listed shares are bought and sold. The dots on the map are %(ex_n)s of them, each in its home city. They come from two published lists: the monthly statistics of the World Federation of Exchanges and the exchange database kept by a United Nations programme. Click or hover on a dot to see how many companies it lists, what they are worth together, how much was traded in a month and which body regulates it. The federation's members reported %(wfe_listed)s listed companies worth %(wfe_mcap)s trillion dollars at the end of September 2026. The value is very unevenly divided. Nasdaq in New York held %(nasdaq)s trillion and the New York Stock Exchange %(nyse)s trillion. Together that is %(us_share)s per cent of the total. Shanghai, Tokyo and Shenzhen follow, at %(sse)s, %(jpx)s and %(szse)s trillion. So the map has many dots, but nearly half of the world's listed value is priced on two exchanges a few streets apart.",
   WFE, pins=[[NY[0], NY[1], "New York"]], pl=["nyse", "nasdaq", "shse", "jpx", "szse", "wfe"], ex=["wfe2026"],
   note="Every exchange in the two sources is drawn as a dot; nothing is left out. The ringed circles are the five largest by the value of their listed companies. The diamond is the World Federation of Exchanges, which publishes the totals. The view opens on the Atlantic, with New York on the left; turn the globe to see Asia. Zoom in where dots overlap, as in New York, Toronto and Mumbai."),
  st("Latest year", "Every country with a figure", WORLD, 1.15, "How Large Each Market Is Beside Its Economy",
   "The value of a listed company is its share price multiplied by the number of its shares. This is called market capitalisation. Add up every listed company in a country and you have the size of its stock market. The World Bank compares that with GDP, which is the value of everything a country produces in a year. The map shades %(mcap_n)s countries. In the United States the market is worth 224 per cent of GDP, in Switzerland 241 and in South Africa 327. In Germany it is 57 per cent, in Brazil 38 and in Nigeria 30. Germany is not a quarter as productive as the United States. Many of its large firms are family-owned or financed by banks and have never listed. So this figure does not measure how rich a country is. It measures how much of its business is organised as traded shares, and how highly those shares are priced. Where the figure is high, the stock market's ups and downs reach further into pensions, pay and politics.",
   WBD + "CM.MKT.LCAP.GD.ZS", data={"k": "mcap", "only": None}, pl=["wfe"]),
 ])

# ================================================================== PART 2 · THE PARTS
flow(id="price", part=1, scope="world",
 title="What a Share Pays, and What Sets Its Price",
 kick="A share pays its owner in two ways, and its price is whatever the last buyer and seller agreed, applied to every share at once.",
 sum="The first chapter said what a share is. This one says why anyone pays for it and how the number on the screen is arrived at. Both are simpler than they are made to sound.<br><br>A share is worth owning for the money it may pay out and for the price someone else may pay for it later. Its quoted price is not an estimate by an expert and not an average. It is the price of the most recent trade. That one fact explains most of what looks mysterious about markets: how a company can gain or lose billions in an afternoon without anything happening inside it, and why prices move on what people expect and not on what has occurred.",
 bridge="The first slide sets out the two ways a share pays. The second shows how one small trade re-prices a whole company. The third explains what buyers believe they are paying for. The fourth maps how often shares change hands in each country.",
 out="A share pays through dividends and resale, its price is set by the latest trade, and buyers are paying for profit that has not happened yet. The next chapter covers the products built on shares.",
 rec="A share's price is the price of the last trade, multiplied across every share, and it reflects profit that buyers expect and not profit that exists.",
 roles=[],
 src=[{"t": "US Securities and Exchange Commission, Investor.gov: Market Capitalization", "u": MCAPDEF},
      {"t": "US Securities and Exchange Commission, Investor.gov: Price-earnings (P/E) Ratio", "u": PEDEF},
      {"t": "S&P Dow Jones Indices, S&P 500 buybacks and dividends, third quarter of 2025 (18 December 2025)", "u": SPBB},
      {"t": "World Bank, Stocks traded, turnover ratio of domestic shares", "u": WBD + "CM.MKT.TRNR"}],
 steps=[
  st("Today", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "Two Ways a Share Pays: Dividends and Resale",
   "There are only two ways to get money out of a share. The first is the dividend. If a company makes a profit, its directors may pay part of it to shareholders, so much per share. The 500 large American companies in the S&P 500 index paid 168.1 billion dollars in dividends in the third quarter of 2025. A company is not obliged to pay one, and many never do. The second way is to sell the share for more than you paid. That is a capital gain, and it depends entirely on finding a buyer at a higher price. Notice where the money comes from in each case. A dividend comes out of the company's profit. A capital gain comes out of another investor's pocket. Most of the return people hope for from shares is the second kind. So the question that runs through the rest of this map is what makes the next buyer willing to pay more.",
   SPBB, w=1, pl=["spdji"], note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country. The ringed circle is S&P Dow Jones Indices in New York, which publishes the dividend total."),
  st("Today", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "The Price Is Set by the Last Few Shares That Traded",
   "The price you see for a share is the price of the most recent trade, and nothing more. The regulator's definition of a company's market value is that price multiplied by all the shares in existence. Follow what that does. A company has 100 million shares. Yesterday the last trade was at 50, so the company was valued at 5 billion. Today 200,000 shares change hands, and the last of them goes at 52. The company is now valued at 5.2 billion. About 10 million of actual money moved, and the value rose by 200 million. The other 99.8 million shares did not trade, but each has been re-priced. This works the same way downward. When a headline says billions were wiped off a market, no such sum was paid by anyone. A small number of shares sold lower, and every other share was marked to match. A price is set by whoever is trading at that moment, however few they are.",
   MCAPDEF, w=1, pl=["sec"], note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country. The numbers in the example are invented for illustration; the rule they follow is the regulator's definition of market capitalisation."),
  st("Today", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "What Buyers Think They Are Paying For: Future Profit",
   "If a price is only the last trade, what are the traders reasoning from? The usual yardstick is profit. Divide a company's yearly profit by its number of shares and you have earnings per share. Divide the share price by that and you have the price-earnings ratio. A share at 50 in a company earning 2.50 a share has a ratio of 20. The buyer is paying twenty years of today's profit. Nobody does that expecting profit to stay the same. They pay it because they expect profit to grow, or because they expect someone else to pay more. So a share price is a forecast with money on it. It rises when the forecast improves and falls when it worsens, whatever this year's profit was. A company can report its best year and see its shares fall, because the buyers had expected better still. The price responds to the gap between what happened and what was expected.",
   PEDEF, w=1, pl=["sec"], note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country. The numbers are invented for illustration."),
  st("Latest year", "Every country with a figure", WORLD, 1.15, "How Often Shares Change Hands",
   "Since the latest trade sets the price, it matters how much trading there is. The World Bank's turnover ratio divides the value of shares traded in a year by the value of the whole market. At 100 per cent, a sum equal to the entire market changed hands in the year. The map shades %(turn_n)s countries. In China the figure for 2024 was 297 per cent and in Brazil 138. In the United States it was 68 and in Germany 45. In Nigeria it was under 3, and %(turn_low)s countries are under 10. Two things follow. Where turnover is very high, the typical share is held for weeks or months, and the owners setting the price are traders, not long-term holders. Where it is very low, days can pass between trades, and the quoted price of a company may rest on a handful of sales. In both cases the number on the screen is a thinner thing than it looks.",
   WBD + "CM.MKT.TRNR", data={"k": "turn", "only": None}, pl=["wfe"]),
 ])

flow(id="products", part=1, scope="world",
 title="The Products: From One Share to a Bet on Thousands",
 kick="Most people never buy a share. They hold a fund that holds shares, tracking a list drawn up by a company they have never heard of.",
 sum="The share is the raw material. Around it an industry has built other things to sell: shares with different voting power, funds that hold hundreds of shares at once, lists that the funds copy, contracts that pay out according to a share's price without the share being owned, and certificates that let a share be traded in another country.<br><br>Each of these has a plain definition. They are worth learning in order, because each later chapter uses them. Indexes return in the chapter on how prices are bent. Funds return in the chapter on rescues. And the gap between owning a share and controlling a company begins here, with the classes of vote.",
 bridge="The first slide covers the kinds of share and their votes. The second explains funds. The third explains the index, the list a fund copies. The fourth explains derivatives, which are bets on a share. The fifth explains how a company's shares come to be traded in a foreign country.",
 out="Shares come with unequal votes, most investors hold them through funds, the funds copy privately made lists, and a layer of bets and certificates sits on top. The next part shows how a company gets into the market in the first place.",
 rec="Most money reaches shares through funds that copy an index, a list made by a private company, and many shares carry little or no say in how a company is run.",
 roles=[],
 src=[{"t": "US Securities and Exchange Commission, Investor.gov: Stocks", "u": STOCKS},
      {"t": "Jay R. Ritter, Initial Public Offerings: Dual Class Structure of IPOs Through 2025", "u": RITDC},
      {"t": "Morningstar, US Fund Flows: Picking Up Steam in 2024", "u": MSTAR},
      {"t": "US Securities and Exchange Commission, Staff Report on Equity and Options Market Structure Conditions in Early 2021", "u": SECGME},
      {"t": "US Securities and Exchange Commission, Investor.gov: American Depositary Receipts", "u": ADR}],
 steps=[
  st("2025", "New York", NY, 3.2, "Ordinary Shares, Preference Shares and Classes of Vote",
   "Not all shares are equal. The regulator's guide names two main kinds. Common stock carries a vote and a right to dividends. Preferred stock usually carries no vote, but its holders are paid their dividend before the common holders get anything. Inside common stock there is a further split that the guide does not dwell on. A company may issue two classes, one with one vote a share for the public and one with many votes a share for its founders. This is a dual-class structure. Professor Ritter's count shows how normal it has become. Of the 90 ordinary company flotations in the United States in 2025, 37 had two classes of share, which is 41 per cent. Among technology companies it was 15 of 31. Across 1980 to 2025 the share was 10 per cent. In such a company the public supplies most of the money and the founders keep most of the votes. Buying its shares buys a claim on profit and almost no say.",
   RITDC, add={"United States": 0}, ex=["ritter"], pl=["sec"]),
  st("2023 to 2024", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "Funds: Buying Many Shares at Once",
   "Choosing single shares is risky, so most savers buy a fund. A fund pools money from many people and buys a mix of shares with it. Each saver owns units of the fund, and the fund owns the shares. There are two styles. In an active fund a manager picks which shares to hold and charges for the judgement. In a passive fund, also called an index fund, nobody picks. The fund simply holds every share on a published list, in the list's proportions. An exchange-traded fund, or ETF, is a fund whose own units are traded on an exchange like a share. Morningstar, a fund research firm, reports that passive funds in the United States overtook active ones in total assets at the end of 2023 and held about 53 per cent a year later. Two consequences follow. The vote attached to each share is cast by the fund manager, not the saver. And over half the fund money in the largest market is now placed without anyone judging the price.",
   MSTAR, w=1, ex=["passive"], note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country. The diamond marks Morningstar in Chicago, whose fund data it draws on."),
  st("Today", "The North Atlantic", [-38, 46], 2.2, "Indexes: The Lists the Funds Copy",
   "An index is a list of shares with a rule for weighting them, boiled down to one number that goes up and down. The S&P 500 is a list of about 500 large American companies. When people say the market rose two per cent, they mean an index did. An index is not a public measurement like the temperature. It is a commercial product. The S&P 500 belongs to S&P Dow Jones Indices in New York. MSCI, also in New York, publishes widely used lists for investing across countries. FTSE Russell in London is the third large provider. Each decides which companies and which countries are on its lists and charges funds for the right to copy them. When Tesla joined the S&P 500 in 2020, press reports put the money tied to that one index at about 11 trillion dollars. Any change to the list makes all of that money buy or sell on the same day. The chapter called The Index Decides shows what that does to prices.",
   TESLA1, pins=[[NY[0], NY[1], "New York"], [LONDON[0], LONDON[1], "London"]], pl=["spdji", "msci", "ftse"], add={"United States": 0, "United Kingdom": 0}),
  st("Today", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "Derivatives: Bets on a Share Without Owning It",
   "A derivative is a contract whose value depends on the price of something else. The commonest one on shares is the option. A call option gives its buyer the right to buy a share at a fixed price before a fixed date. Suppose a share trades at 100. You pay 2 for the right to buy it at 105 within a month. If the share rises to 115, your right is worth 10, five times what you paid. If the share ends below 105, the right is worthless and your 2 is gone. A put option is the reverse, a right to sell. The buyer of either is making a magnified bet with a small stake. The seller, usually a trading firm, carries the other side and often buys or sells the actual share to protect itself. That is how bets feed back into the share price. The American regulator's staff report on the trading of early 2021 describes this market and the part options played in that episode.",
   SECGME, w=1, pl=["sec"], ex=["secgme"], note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country. The numbers are invented for illustration. The diamond marks the regulator's staff report, used here for its description of the options market."),
  st("September 2026", "New York", NY, 2.4, "Depositary Receipts: A Share Traded in Another Country",
   "A company's shares can trade far from where the company is. One way is to list directly on a foreign exchange. Another is the depositary receipt. A bank holds a block of a foreign company's shares and issues certificates against them, and the certificates trade locally in local currency. In the United States these are called American depositary receipts. The exchange figures show how far this goes. Of the %(nasdaq_tot)s companies listed on Nasdaq in September 2026, %(nasdaq_for)s were foreign. On the New York Stock Exchange %(nyse_for)s of %(nyse_tot)s were. So a company can make its goods in one country, be registered for legal purposes in a second, and have its price set in a third. Which country's rules protect the shareholder then becomes a real question, and the answer is usually the country where the shares trade, for the trading, and the country of registration, for the votes. A later slide maps the regulator behind each exchange.",
   WFE, add={"United States": 0}, pl=["nyse", "nasdaq"], ex=["wfe2026"]),
 ])

# ================================================================== PART 3 · GETTING IN
flow(id="ipo", part=2, scope="world",
 title="The IPO: The Day a Company Sells Shares to the Public",
 kick="On the day a company first sells shares, a bank sets the price, chooses who may buy, and the first buyers gain about a fifth by evening.",
 sum="A company is not born listed. It starts private, owned by its founders and a few backers. To reach the stock market it sells shares to the public for the first time. This is the initial public offering, or IPO, also called a flotation.<br><br>This is the one moment when the company itself is paid. It is worth watching closely, because the price is not set by an open market. It is set beforehand, in private, by the investment banks the company hires, and the same banks decide who is allowed to buy at that price. What happens on the first day of trading shows who that arrangement serves.",
 bridge="The first slide explains why a company lists and how many have stopped doing so. The second explains the underwriter, the bank that sets the price. The third measures the first-day jump over forty-five years. The fourth is one very large flotation outside the United States.",
 out="A flotation is priced in private by banks, the shares go to buyers the banks choose, and the first-day jump is money the company did not receive. The next chapter covers the other routes in and the exchanges that host them.",
 rec="At a flotation the bank sets the price and picks the buyers, and since 1980 American companies have left about 21 cents on the table for every dollar they raised.",
 roles=["Where the flotation described took place"],
 src=[{"t": "Jay R. Ritter, Initial Public Offerings: Underpricing (Table 1, updated 24 December 2025)", "u": RIT},
      {"t": "National Bureau of Economic Research Digest, Why Are There So Few Public Companies in the U.S.? (Doidge, Karolyi and Stulz)", "u": DKS},
      {"t": "Bloomberg News, Software Maker Figma Surges 250% in Debut After $1.2 Billion IPO (31 July 2025)", "u": FIGMA},
      {"t": "CNN, Saudi Aramco raises $25.6 billion in the world's biggest IPO (5 December 2019)", "u": ARAMCO}],
 steps=[
  st("1996 to 2012", "United States", [-96, 39], 2.6, "Why a Company Lists, and Why Many No Longer Do",
   "A company lists for three reasons. It raises money without borrowing. Its early owners get a way to sell. And it gains shares it can use like currency, to pay staff or buy other firms. In return it must publish its accounts, report every quarter and accept that anyone may buy in. For a long time listing was treated as the natural destination of a successful firm. In the United States that has stopped being true. The economists Doidge, Karolyi and Stulz counted about 8,000 listed American companies in 1996 and about 4,100 in 2012, while the rest of the world went from 30,700 to 39,400. Companies were bought by others, or stayed private and took money from private funds. The practical effect is on who gets in early. When a company grows large in private hands, its fastest growth is over before the public is offered a share. The public market gets the company late, at a higher price.",
   DKS, add={"United States": 0}, ex=["dks"]),
  st("Today", "New York", NY, 3.4, "The Underwriter Sets the Price and Picks the Buyers",
   "A company cannot simply open a stall. It hires investment banks, called underwriters, to run the sale. The underwriters take the company's managers to meet large investors, ask how many shares each would take and at what price, and from those conversations fix one offer price the evening before trading begins. They then hand out the shares. This step decides who profits. The shares do not go to whoever asks first. The banks allocate them, mostly to large funds that are their regular customers, and take a fee from the company as a percentage of the money raised. A member of the public usually cannot buy at the offer price at all. They can buy only once trading opens, from someone who was allocated shares. So the bank serves two sides at once: the company, which wants a high price, and the investing clients it deals with every day, who want a low one. The next slide shows which side has done better.",
   RIT, add={"United States": 0}, ex=["ritter"]),
  st("1980 to 2025", "United States", [-96, 39], 2.6, "The First-Day Jump: Money Left on the Table",
   "Professor Ritter has recorded the first day of every ordinary American flotation since 1980. Across 9,343 of them to the end of 2025, the share price closed on average 19 per cent above the offer price on the first day. In 2025 the average was 29 per cent. When the design software firm Figma floated on 31 July 2025, its shares rose 250 per cent by the close. A first-day jump means the shares were sold for less than buyers were willing to pay hours later. Ritter multiplies each jump by the number of shares sold and calls the result money left on the table. From 1980 to 2025 it comes to 250 billion dollars, against 1,190 billion raised. That is about 21 cents for every dollar the companies received. The money did not vanish. It went to the investors the underwriters had chosen. The company and its original owners sold a slice of the business for less than the market's price, and paid a fee for the service.",
   RIT, add={"United States": 0}, ex=["ritter"], pins=[[SF[0], SF[1], "Figma, San Francisco"]]),
  st("2019", "Riyadh", RIYADH, 3.2, "Regional Example: Saudi Arabia Floats Its Oil Company at Home",
   "Regional Example: The largest flotation on record was not in New York or London. In December 2019 Saudi Arabia sold shares in its state oil company, Saudi Aramco, on the Saudi exchange in Riyadh. CNN reported that it raised 25.6 billion dollars, more than any flotation before it. The seller was a government, and the venue was its own national exchange, which the map shows is supervised by the kingdom's Capital Market Authority. This is the same mechanism as any other flotation: a slice of an existing business is priced by banks and sold once. But it shows that listing is also a tool of states. A government can turn part of a national asset into cash, choose the exchange whose rules it writes, and give its home market a very large company. The exchange dot for Riyadh shows what the Saudi market now lists and what it is worth.",
   ARAMCO, add={"Saudi Arabia": 0}, reg=True, pl=["tadawul"]),
 ])

flow(id="doors", part=2, scope="world",
 title="Other Ways In, and the Exchanges That Compete to Host Them",
 kick="A company can reach the market without a flotation, and the exchange it lands on is itself a company selling a service.",
 sum="The flotation is the best-known way onto the market, but there are two others worth knowing, and both were sold as cures for its faults. One removes the bank's price-setting. The other removes most of the scrutiny.<br><br>Then there is the place itself. An exchange sounds like a public institution, and many once were clubs owned by their members. Most are now businesses with shareholders of their own. They earn fees from each company they list and each trade they host, and they compete with one another for both. The regulator standing behind each exchange differs from country to country, and no body sits above them all.",
 bridge="The first slide explains the direct listing. The second explains the SPAC, a listed shell that buys a company. The third shows that most exchanges are now businesses. The fourth shows who regulates them.",
 out="There are three doors into the market, the widest in 2021 was the one with the least scrutiny, and the exchanges behind the doors are companies competing for custom under national regulators. The next part follows a single trade.",
 rec="A company can list through a flotation, a direct listing or a shell company, and it chooses its exchange, and so its regulator, from businesses competing for the fee.",
 roles=["Where the listing described took place"],
 src=[{"t": "BBC News, Is Spotify really worth $23bn? (1 March 2018)", "u": SPOT},
      {"t": "Jay R. Ritter, Initial Public Offerings: SPACs (Table 15b)", "u": RITSP},
      {"t": "M. Klausner, M. Ohlrogge and E. Ruan, A Sober Look at SPACs, Yale Journal on Regulation 39 (2022)", "u": SOBER},
      {"t": "UN Sustainable Stock Exchanges initiative, Stock Exchange Database", "u": SSE},
      {"t": "International Organization of Securities Commissions, About IOSCO", "u": IOSCO}],
 steps=[
  st("2018", "New York", NY, 3.2, "Direct Listing: No New Shares and No Bank Setting the Price",
   "In April 2018 the music streaming company Spotify, founded in Stockholm, arrived on the New York Stock Exchange without a flotation. The BBC described the method beforehand: the firm would list its shares directly, and instead of issuing new shares, its existing shareholders would take theirs straight to the market. This is a direct listing. No bank fixes an offer price the night before and no bank allocates shares to its clients. Trading simply opens, and the first price is whatever buyers and sellers agree. There is no first-day jump to give away, because nothing was sold beforehand at a lower price. The limit of the method, in that form, is that the company raises no money. Only existing owners sell. It suits a firm that is already well funded and well known. The flotation remains the usual route all the same: Professor Ritter counted 90 of them in the United States in 2025.",
   SPOT, add={"United States": 0}, arcs=[[STOCKHOLM, NY]], an=[("Stockholm", "New York")], pl=["nyse"]),
  st("2019 to 2021", "United States", [-96, 39], 2.6, "The SPAC: A Listed Shell That Buys a Company",
   "A special purpose acquisition company, or SPAC, is a company with no business. Its organisers, called sponsors, float it at 10 dollars a share and promise to use the cash to buy a private company within about two years. When it does, the private company takes over the shell's listing and is public without a flotation of its own. In 2021 there were 613 SPAC flotations in the United States raising 144.5 billion dollars, against 311 flotations of ordinary companies. Three law professors, Klausner, Ohlrogge and Ruan, examined what was left of each 10 dollars by the time of the merger. For SPACs that merged between January 2019 and June 2020, the median was 5.70 in cash per share. The rest had gone on the sponsors' free shares, fees and payouts to investors who left early. They found that the cost fell on the shareholders who stayed through the merger, who then suffered steep losses, while sponsors profited. The route with the lightest checks was the most crowded.",
   SOBER, add={"United States": 0}, ex=["spac"]),
  st("Today", "Every exchange in the database", WORLD, 1.15, "Exchanges Are Businesses Competing for Listings",
   "For most of their history exchanges were clubs. The brokers who traded on them owned them and ran them for their own convenience. Turning such a club into a company with shareholders is called demutualisation. The United Nations database records how each exchange describes its own ownership. Of the %(model_n)s exchanges that give a description, %(model_demut)s use the words listed or demutualised. Nasdaq is entered as a listed company for profit. So is the New York Stock Exchange, demutualised in 2006. An exchange of this kind earns money from companies that pay to be listed, from each trade, and from selling its price data. It therefore wants more listings and more trading. It is also the body that writes the listing rules, such as whether a company with two classes of vote may join. A company choosing between exchanges is a customer choosing between suppliers, and the supplier writes the rules it will be held to. Click any dot to read its ownership entry.",
   SSE, w=1, pl=["nyse", "nasdaq", "lseg", "euronext", "dboerse", "hkex", "jpx"], ex=["sse_db"],
   note="Every exchange in the two sources is drawn as a dot. The ringed circles are seven large exchange groups that are themselves listed companies. The diamond marks the UN programme in Geneva that keeps the database. The count is of entries whose ownership description contains the word listed or demutualised, in any spelling."),
  st("Today", "Every exchange in the database", WORLD, 1.15, "Who Watches Each Exchange",
   "Every exchange answers to a regulator, and the dots on the map name it for %(reg_n)s of the %(ex_n)s. In the United States it is the Securities and Exchange Commission. In China it is the China Securities Regulatory Commission. In India it is the Securities and Exchange Board of India. In several countries, among them Armenia, Bahrain and Russia, the database names the central bank. These bodies approve listings, police fraud and can halt trading. Each one's authority stops at its border. The closest thing to a world body is the International Organization of Securities Commissions in Madrid, a club of regulators. It says its members regulate more than 99 per cent of the world's securities markets in more than 130 jurisdictions. It sets standards, but it cannot order anyone to do anything. So a company that can list anywhere is also choosing which rulebook and which enforcer it will face, and a share traded in three countries has three watchers, each looking at a part.",
   IOSCO, w=1, pins=[[MADRID[0], MADRID[1], "IOSCO, Madrid"]], pl=["iosco", "sec", "csrc", "sebi", "esma", "fsaj", "fca"],
   note="Every exchange in the two sources is drawn as a dot, and its regulator is given where the UN database names one. The ringed circles are the world body of regulators and six national or regional regulators."),
 ])
